(project pricklypear-agent
 (doc "Pricklypear book chapter: agent — a Nopales-native local coding agent, pi-inspired, sharing pricklypear-lisp with the image")
 (status planned)
 (* agent note (|2026-08-03 chapter created for the pi-clone spike. Positioning: sibling consumer of pricklypear-lisp (like cli/), NOT part of the image product. No Postgres, no branches, no auth. If it graduates into the product later that is a plan-phase spec edit.|) *)
 (* agent note (|2026-08-14 v0.0 spike landed: rim moved to Control.run_with_rim (image Control_rim is now a thin alias), Eval.read_eval_all_direct added, agent/ package with stdin REPL + mock provider + read/bash builtins + on/register-tool/register-command + tool_call-block path. Provider is OpenAI-compatible chat completions only (synthetic.new + any gateway, Anthropic via its compat endpoint) — no native Anthropic path; image llm_client.ml is the curl cautionary tale we avoid. agent/ tests 5/5 (incl. provider SSE reconstruction), full workspace green. Known gaps: SSE streaming now lands text deltas live (Openai.complete streams requests with stream:true; text deltas dispatch via on_delta as they decode) but tool-call stream fragments are RECONSTRUCTED into a full assistant message (not streamed live as tool-call deltas) — that reconstruction runs through the shared parse_reply so XML/GLM fallback and history shape are preserved; http_ui.ml landed: HTML primary surface (chat page + inline SSE client), code browser (read-only file tree + file view), --serve/--open wired in main.ml. Chat streams via true incremental SSE (producer-fiber + drain queue, mirroring image http_agent.ml mp/sse pattern). Gluten_eio deadlock avoided by forking handler work off the connection fiber. Code browser has canonical-path traversal guard. All surfaces smokes pass (chat page, SSE deltas, tree list, file render, traversal 403, piped-turn regression).|) *)
 (* agent note (|2026-08-14 SURFACE REFACTOR DESIGN (decided, plan-mode): the agent UI moves off SSE+vanilla-JS to Nopales-authored htmx fragments + multipart streaming, so the surface is a loadable Nopales layer both the agent AND the image share. Decision thread: (1) soup/* tree prims are DB-free — lift them from image/lib/prims_hyper.ml into the shared pure-prims layer (pure/) so both hosts register the same tree prims; (2) route/lookup + route/dispatch are THE HOST SEAM (hypermedia-harness.request-runner) — identical Nopales signatures, host-local backing (image Session.t Postgres routes vs agent Runtime.t lens routes); (3) LLM use is always-API (no local inference), so the main habitat stays the corpus source of truth; the agent evaluates against a LOCAL-FIRST LENS (local-first.borg), not a hermetic embedded corpus — edits sync up per local-first.lens-model. This revises the 2026-08-03 positioning which said 'no Postgres, no branches, no object store' and made the corpus embeddable. Streaming stays OCAML (provider SSE -> deltas -> per-part (html,target,swap) assembly -> multipart frame + flush), with Nopales owning the fragment templates; soup/apply-parts! models a consumed turn as data for libs/hyper. Phasing (read-only lens vs write-capable lens in the first cut) was left OPEN in discussion — record it in local-first.phasing when decided.|) *)
 (* agent note (|2026-12 CAMPAIGN 6.0 PLAN PASS (agent surfaces / route table spec): revealed the agent-route-table + surfaces implementation was ALREADY COMPLETE in agent/ (built since the 2026-08 design). Reconciled both stanzas' verify stanzas + details to the implemented reality WITHOUT a status flip (per the plan-pass/make division of labour — 6.1 make flips them planned → implemented). Verified live: agent_test 13/13 green; smoke_agent_surface.sh 14/14 PASS; piped-turn `--once` + `-p` pipe mode both work. agent-route-table = route / route/lookup / route/dispatch + thin request context (query-param / request-method / hx-request?) in agent/lib/runtime.ml, the host seam libs/hyper runs on unchanged; surfaces = Nopales-authored htmx fragments + OCaml multipart stream + GENERATED assets.ml/libs_source.ml (dune-produced; must stay in the surfaces (implements …) list to cover the modules at build — their real sources are libs/html/lib.pp + libs/hyper/lib.pp + image/static/*.js). No code changes needed — a spec-reality reconcile only. REMAINING for 6.1 make: flip both stanzas to implemented; re-run borge drift (note the generated-module wrinkle: implements-exist sees assets.ml/libs_source.ml as missing on an IMPLEMENTED stanza because they only exist under _build — an open borge modeling limit worth an issue if 6.1 can't reconcile); formalize smoke_agent_surface.sh into the verify apparatus; wire the local-first lens backing (a campaign-8 dependency, not strictly 6.x).|) *)
 (convention ocaml-dune)
 (section agent
  (doc "A local single-user coding agent binary: minimal agent loop, Nopales plugins, code browser. Launched in a directory: `cd project && agent`. pi (pi.dev) is the reference point; the differentiator is that plugins are Nopales loaded into a live env shared with the session REPL, so the user redefines plugin functions while the agent runs. Stack shares with the image where trivially possible: pricklypear-lisp (eval), Eio rim style (synchronous prim ABI, fiber-blocking I/O), pure-html for the web surface.")

  (subsection positioning
   (doc "What the agent is and is not, relative to the pricklypear image")
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
         IS:
           - A single native OCaml binary, installable/runnable from a
             laptop checkout: `agent` in any directory opens a session
             on that directory.
           - Single-user, localhost. One operator, one working copy.
           - A LOCAL-FIRST HOST grounded in the habitat. The Nopales
             corpus (function defs, packages, route table) is a
             local-first LENS of the one tree (local-first.borg):
             authoritative on the main habitat, materialized locally
             per local-first.lens-model, edits syncing up per
             local-first.sync. This REVISES the 2026-08-03 spike
             position, which scoped the agent to 'no Postgres, no
             branches, no object store' and embedded the corpus.
           - A second home for Nopales: plugin files under
             ~/.config/agent/plugins/*.pp (global) and
             .agent/plugins/*.pp (project) are evaluated into one env
             shared with the interactive session REPL. Live
             redefinition of plugin functions takes effect on the
             agent's next turn.

         IS NOT:
           - Part of the image. The image remains the multi-user
             habitat product (single-image.borg) and the corpus
             authority; the agent is a sibling CLIENT host, not a
             second source of truth.
           - An independent corpus. The 2026-08-03 'no Postgres/
             branches/store' boundary is revised: the agent's store
             is a governed LENS, not a private embedded corpus
             (local-first.borg positioning wrote the 2026-08-03
             hard-out against an UNCOORDINATED copy; a lens is a
             governed branch with merge semantics).
           - A Nopales fork. Language semantics come from
             pricklypear-lisp unchanged; the agent adds HOST PRIMS and
             an optional host library layer in .pp, and nothing else.
             If the agent needs a language change, that change lands
             via lisp-interpreter.borg like any other.
           - pi's TUI. The primary surface is HTML (pure-html +
             htmx, multipart for streams); a minimal line REPL mode
             exists for scripts and embedding, not as the primary
             interaction.
    |))))

  (subsection nopales-embed
   (doc "Embedding pricklypear-lisp as the host of the plugin environment")
   (status partial)
   (implements lisp/lib/control.ml lisp/lib/eval.ml agent/lib/runtime.ml)
   (verify
    (test "a .pp plugin file defining an event handler is loaded at startup and its handler fires on the agent loop's tool_call event")
    (test "redefining the same handler by evaluating a new definition in the session REPL changes subsequent tool_call behavior without restart")
    (test "an unregistered prim call from a plugin produces a structured Interp_error, not an Unhandled effect escape")
    (test "two evals never interleave on shared Eval global state (serialize via mutex); a nested plugin-triggered eval during an active turn completes correctly"))
   (details
    (doc (|
         SEAM. pricklypear-lisp is already the embeddable artifact:
         Eval.eval_string_direct ~prims env s plus a pure prim table
         and Control.Prim_call effects. The agent provides a rim in
         the style of image Control_rim: an Effect.Deep handler over
         Prim_call dispatching to the host table, with
         Control.direct_prims set during prim bodies so nested eval
         dispatches directly.

         MOVE DOWN. The rim handler (currently image-only) moves into
         lisp/lib/control.ml as Control.run_with_rim so both hosts
         share it. Mechanical move, no behavior change; the image
         keeps a thin alias until call sites are updated.

         CONCURRENCY MODEL. All Nopales evaluation is serialized
         through one Eio mutex. Eval's global refs (current_budget,
         last_budget) stay global in v0; per-eval threading is a
         follow-on only if real pain appears. Nested eval triggered by
         a plugin callback from inside a tool handler re-enters
         through direct_prims under the same budget, as the image
         rim already does.

         PRIM ABI. Prims are synchronous (value list -> value). Host
         prims block the calling fiber (Eio), never Lwt: the agent
         has no PP_IO switch and no lwt rollback path. plugin-authored
         Nopales code therefore looks fully synchronous even when a
         prim performs network I/O.

         SHARED soup/ KERNEL. The soup/ tree prims (hypermedia-
         harness.kernel-prims: soup/parse, select, swap!, apply-parts!,
         diff, closest-attr, …) are DB-free and registered here from
         the SAME shared pure-prims layer the image uses (lifted from
         image/lib/prims_hyper.ml into pure/). html/ (the pure-html DSL
         for composing fragments) is a NOPALES package (libs/html),
         not an OCaml prim layer — the agent loads it as source
         against these prims, like libs/hyper. lambdasoup + markup
         become agent deps, joined on the same seam.

         HOST PRIM NAMESPACE. Agent prims are prefixed by family:
           ui/notify ui/confirm ui/select        (browser surfaces)
           session/append session/name
           on register-tool register-command     (plugin API)
           http/get http/post                    (Eio; JSON in/out)
           route route/lookup route/dispatch     (agent-route-table;
                                                  THE HOST SEAM shared
                                                  with hypermedia-harness)
           soup/*                                (shared tree prims,
                                                  hypermedia-harness)
         Pure language prims are untouched. The image's DB/auth/
         session prims do not exist here; route prims are the
         agent's LOCAL-LENS backing, not Postgres.
    |))))

  (subsection agent-loop
   (doc "The minimal agent turn loop and tool registry")
   (status partial)
   (implements agent/lib/loop.ml agent/lib/provider.ml agent/lib/tools.ml common/lib/llm_codec.ml)
   (verify
    (test "a mock provider emitting one tool call round-trips: tool executes, result message is appended, loop makes exactly one follow-up model call")
    (test "budget: the loop stops after a configurable max turns and reports rather than spinning")
    (test "tool_call plugin hook can block a call; the model sees the block reason as the tool result")
    (smoke "openai-compat provider streams a real reply end-to-end when AGENT_API_KEY is set"))
   (details
    (doc (|
         SHAPE. One assistant turn = one streaming provider call. If
         the reply contains tool calls, execute them (serialized eval
         mutex held only during each tool's Nopales work), append
         results, call again. Max-turns guard, abort signal, and
         usage accounting per turn.

         PROVIDERS v0. OpenAI-compatible chat completions HTTP (covers
         synthetic.new and every gateway we use, Anthropic included
         via its compat endpoint) over Httpun_eio... hand-rolled 1.1
         POST because gluten refuses non-socket TLS flows. No curl
         subprocesses (image llm_client.ml is the cautionary tale),
         no native Anthropic messages path. SSE parsing in-process
         once streaming lands.

         TOOL REGISTRY v0. read, bash, edit — matching the pi
         baseline shapes so prompts and habits transfer. bash runs
         via Eio.Process with timeout and output truncation.

         TOOL SCHEMAS. Tool parameter schemas are data (yojson)
         constructed by the registry, serializable for both provider
         wire formats. register-tool accepts a Nopales alist schema
         and a Nopales closure body; the closure runs under the rim
         inside the loop.

         PROVIDER ABSTRACTION. A variant type provider_reply with
         text/tool-call/delta constructors; each provider module maps
         wire events to it. Adding a provider = one module, no loop
         changes.
    |))))

  (subsection plugin-model
   (doc "Nopales plugin API: events, custom tools, commands, session REPL")
   (status partial)
   (implements agent/lib/plugins.ml agent/test/agent_test.ml agent/bin/main.ml)
   (verify
    (test "loading order: global plugins then project plugins; a project plugin can redefine a global binding")
    (test "on/2 registers a handler; handlers for one event run in registration order; tool_call block short-circuits remaining handlers")
    (test "register-tool closures appear in the provider tool list on the turn after registration")
    (test "register-command commands are intercepted before the input reaches the model"))
   (details
    (doc (|
         FILES. ~/.config/agent/plugins/*.pp then .agent/plugins/*.pp
         (cwd), each evaluated in filename order into the shared env.
         A plugin file is ordinary Nopales; no module ceremony.

         EVENTS v0 (subset of pi's surface, deliberately small):
           tool_call   (may block: return (dict "block" #t "reason" s))
           tool_result (may replace content)
           input       (may transform text or handle outright)
           before_turn (may inject a context message)
         Event payloads are JSON strings manipulated with the frozen
         dict-* prims — no new value types.

         CUSTOM TOOLS. (register-tool name params-alist closure).
         Params alist maps param name -> "string"|"int"|"bool" with
         optional "description" entries; registry builds JSON schema.
         Closure receives one JSON string argument.

         COMMANDS. (register-command name doc closure). `/name args`
         in the input line is dispatched to the closure before model
         submission.

         REPL UNITY. The session REPL (stdin mode and browser /repl
         pane) is the same env. `(define ...)` at the prompt
         redefines handler functions live; there is no reload
         command because there is nothing to reload.

         SAFETY. Plugin code runs with the user's full permissions
         (same trust posture as pi). Project plugins load only after
         an interactive trust confirmation; the decision is cached
         per directory hash under ~/.config/agent/trust.json.
    |))))

  (subsection agent-route-table
   (doc "The Nopales route table: route, route/lookup, route/dispatch — THE HOST SEAM between the agent and the image (hypermedia-harness.request-runner). Identical Nopales signatures, host-local backing. Routes bind a path to a Nopales render handler the same way the image's (route ...) does.")
   (status planned)
   (implements agent/lib/runtime.ml)
   (verify
    (test "agent-route-table / register-and-render: a (route \"/chat\" \"chat/render\") registered by a plugin makes /chat resolvable via route/lookup and renderable via route/dispatch (which invokes the handler through Runtime.apply_value under the eval rim and returns the rendered raw-HTML VStr)")
    (test "agent-route-table / thin-context: route/dispatch binds a thin request context so query-param / request-method / hx-request? resolve inside the handler")
    (test "agent-route-table / pg-prim-unbound: a handler calling a Postgres-only request prim (e.g. current-author as a DB row) errors with unbound-prim in the agent — the agent's request context carries only request-shape values"))
   (details
    (doc (|
         IMPLEMENTED + VERIFIED (campaign 6.0 plan pass, 2026-12 — status
         STAYS planned pending the 6.1 make flip, per the plan pass/make
         division of labour). The whole route-table seam is live in
         agent/lib/runtime.ml and covered by the agent suite's
         agent-route-table group (3 tests, all green): (route path
         handler) registers into the Runtime route Hashtbl;
         (route/lookup path) resolves path -> handler-name VStr | VNil;
         (route/dispatch handler method path [params] [headers] [body])
         invokes the handler under the eval rim + mutex with a THIN REQUEST
         CONTEXT bound (query-param / request-method / hx-request?;
         DB/branch/auth request prims stay UNBOUND, so libs/hyper runs
         unchanged on both hosts). dispatch_route is the locking wrapper
         the HTTP gate uses; dispatch_route_raw is the shared in-eval
         body. render is via Prims_soup.render_handler_value (the shared
         soup kernel lifted into pure/). The request context also carries
         session/last-turn-parts (surfaces scope) so a consumed turn
         replays through hyper/stream.

         SHAPE. Runtime.t gains a route table (path -> handler-name).
         Nopales registers routes with the same (route path handler)
         shape the image uses; route/lookup resolves a path to its
         handler; route/dispatch invokes the handler in-process
         through Runtime.apply_value (under the eval rim + mutex) with
         a THIN REQUEST CONTEXT bound and returns the rendered
         raw-HTML VStr.

         SAME SEMANTICS, LOCAL BACKING. The image backs these prims
         with Session.t routes over Postgres + a fat Request_context
         with manifest/store/branches. The agent backs them with the
         Runtime route table + a thinner request context — the SAME
         Nopales signatures, so libs/hyper and the surface handlers
         run unchanged on both hosts. The route table is where the
         corpus's routes are materialized; as a local-first host the
         corpus (incl. defs) comes from the lens, matching the
         positioning subsection.

         ROUTE INSTANCES. Surface pages (/ /chat and the code
         browser) register as route-table routes in Nopales; the
         multipart STREAM emission for a chat/agent turn stays OCaml
         (surfaces subsection) — a route's handler initiates a host
         stream rather than returning a single fragment for streaming
         routes.
    |)))
   (details
    (doc (|
         SHAPE. Runtime.t gains a route table (path -> handler-name).
         Nopales registers routes with the same (route path handler)
         shape the image uses; route/lookup resolves a path to its
         handler; route/dispatch invokes the handler in-process
         through Runtime.apply_value (under the eval rim + mutex) with
         a THIN REQUEST CONTEXT bound (query-param, request-method,
         hx-request? resolve; DB/branch/auth request prims are
         unbound) and returns the rendered raw-HTML VStr.

         SAME SEMANTICS, LOCAL BACKING. The image backs these prims
         with Session.t routes over Postgres + a fat Request_context
         with manifest/store/branches. The agent backs them with the
         Runtime route table + a thinner request context — the SAME
         Nopales signatures, so libs/hyper and the surface handlers
         run unchanged on both hosts (hypermedia-harness.request-
         runner). The route table is where the corpus's routes are
         materialized; as a local-first host the corpus (incl. defs)
         comes from the lens, matching the positioning subsection.

         ROUTE INSTANCES. Surface pages (/ /chat /and the code
         browser) register as route-table routes in Nopales; the
         multipart STREAM emission for a chat/agent turn stays OCaml
         (surfaces subsection) — a route's handler initiates a host
         stream rather than returning a single fragment for streaming
         routes.
    |))))

  (subsection surfaces
   (doc "Interaction surfaces: HTML primary, line REPL secondary. The chat/agent surface is NOPALES-AUTHORED htmx fragments + multipart streaming; the stream is emitted by OCaml, the fragments by Nopales.")
   (status planned)
   (implements agent/lib/http_ui.ml agent/lib/runtime.ml agent/lib/assets.ml agent/lib/surface.ml agent/lib/libs_source.ml libs/html/lib.pp libs/hyper/lib.pp)
   (verify
    (smoke "surfaces / nopales-routes: agent boots and serves the NOPALES-authored chat + code pages on the route table (/ and /code), redefinable from the session REPL without restart")
    (smoke "surfaces / turn-replay-hyper-stream + turn-replay-nested-blocks: a consumed chat turn replays deterministically through libs/hyper (hyper/stream over the captured part list) in the agent host")
    (smoke "scripts/smoke_agent_surface.sh (end-to-end): boots `_build/default/agent/bin/main.exe --serve --mock-script`, GET / serves the hx-post=/chat form + hx-multipart asset, GET /assets/hx-multipart.js is 200, POST /chat streams multipart/mixed chunked with all parts + close boundary and TERMINATES promptly, GET /code renders the file tree")
    (smoke "surfaces line REPL / piped turn: `echo PROMPT | agent --once --mock-script m.json` runs one full turn from piped stdin and prints the assistant reply; `agent -p EXPR` evals one Nopales expr and exits"))
   (details
    (doc (|
         PLAN-PASS RECONCILE (campaign 6.0, 2026-12 — status STAYS
         planned pending the 6.1 make flip). All five surfaces smokes are
         live and green: the agent suite's surfaces group (nopales-routes,
         turn-replay-hyper-stream, turn-replay-nested-blocks — 3 tests)
         exercises the Nopales-authored routes + deterministic hyper/stream
         replay; scripts/smoke_agent_surface.sh boots the real binary with
         --serve + --mock-script and proves the chat page, the
         hx-multipart asset, the /chat multipart/mixed chunked stream
         (all parts + close boundary + prompt termination), and the
         /code file tree over HTTP/1.1 keep-alive (14 checks, PASS);
         the line-REPL smokes run via `agent --once --mock-script`
         (piped-turn) and `agent -p EXPR` (pipe mode) — both verified.
         The vendored htmx assets and embedded libs/html + libs/hyper
         sources are GENERATED module slices (dune rules producing
         agent/lib/assets.ml and agent/lib/libs_source.ml into _build),
         so 6.1 must keep those two paths in this stanza's (implements …)
         to cover the dune modules at build, while their real sources
         are libs/html/lib.pp + libs/hyper/lib.pp + image/static/*.js.

         HTML PRIMARY, NOPALES SURFACE. Local HTTP server (Httpun_eio)
         on 127.0.0.1:random-port; prints the URL (and --open to
         launch a browser). Chat and the code browser are htmx
         fragment routes on the agent route table (agent-route-
         table), rendered by Nopales via libs/html — NOT compiled
         OCaml pages and NOT vanilla-JS SSE. The surface is
         redefinable live (same env as plugins/REPL).

         STREAMING IS OCAML. A chat/agent turn's stream is a compiled
         host concern: provider SSE -> delta assembly -> per-part
         (html, HX-Target, HX-Swap) assembly (delegating only the
         fragment TEMPLATE to a Nopales render fn) -> multipart/mixed
         frame + flush over the producer-fiber/drain-queue plumbing
         already proven in the image and the v0.0 agent.
         Nopales owns the page skeleton and fragment templates;
         OCaml owns the effects-coloured stream. The parts are ALSO
         collected as data (session/last-turn-parts) so libs/hyper can
         replay a consumed turn deterministically (soup/apply-parts!,
         hyper/stream).

         LINE REPL. stdin/stdout mode for scripts, embedding, and
         tty diehards. Same env, same prims, no htmx. Piped-turn
         mode (--once / non-tty stdin) reads the whole of stdin as one
         prompt, runs a single full turn, prints the final assistant
         text, exits 0.

         GUI. Explicitly out. Nothing in the feature list needs
         native widgets, and a second UI ecosystem doubles the
         maintenance surface.

         UI PRIMS. ui/confirm, ui/select, ui/notify resolve against
         the active surface: modal/banner component in the browser,
         y/n prompt on stdin in REPL mode. A blocking ui/confirm in
         an event handler suspends only the agent fiber; the HTTP
         fiber keeps streaming because prims block fibers, not
         threads (Eio), and eval serialization is per-eval, not
         per-process-park.
    |))))

  (subsection v0-non-goals
   (doc "Explicit exclusions for the first working version")
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
         OUT for v0, even though pi has them:
           - Session trees / fork / resume (sessions are one JSONL
             file per directory under .agent/sessions/, appended only)
           - Compaction hooks, custom TUI widgets, theme system
           - Provider discovery / model registry beyond a
             ~/.config/agent/config.toml table
           - Sandboxing of any kind (bash is bash)
           - Sharing sessions across machines

         HARD OUT (forever, or a different project):
           - Postgres or any server-side store
           - Multi-user, auth, branches, promote
           - Lwt
           - Language changes made in agent/ instead of through
             lisp-interpreter.borg
    |))))
 )
)
